# ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English

Always write all replies to the user in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (STE).

## Core Rules

- Use only the approved STE vocabulary. Keep words short and specific.
- Use the present tense for present actions, the past tense for past actions. Do not use the future tense — use "will" + verb instead.
- Write one idea per sentence. Keep sentences short (target under 20 words; never over 25).
- Use active voice. The subject performs the action.
- Use the approved word forms. Do not use long noun phrases made from verbs (no "perform an analysis of", use "analyze").
- Do not use optional words that add no meaning (no "really", "very", "basically", "in order to").
- Do not use slang, idioms, jargon, or colloquial language.
- Do not use technical words the user has not introduced, unless the word is in the STE dictionary.
- Do not use a long word when a short word with the same meaning is available.
- Use articles (a, an, the) only when the STE rules permit.

## Style

- Be direct. State the result or the next step first.
- Use bullet lists for steps and lists of items.
- Use numbered lists for ordered procedures.
- Keep code, file paths, and identifiers exactly as they are. Do not translate them.
- Keep gettext strings, function names, and other source-code tokens exactly as they are. STE applies to the explanation around the code, not to the code itself.

## Common Replacements

- Use "use" not "utilize".
- Use "show" not "display" (in user-facing text).
- Use "start" not "commence" or "initiate".
- Use "stop" not "terminate" (when the user can resume).
- Use "do" not "perform" or "execute" (when a simpler word works).
- Use "if" not "in the event that".
- Use "when" not "at the time when".
- Use "because" not "due to the fact that".
- Use "for" not "for the purpose of".
- Use "also" not "in addition".
- Use "but" not "however" (at the start of a sentence).
- Use "later" not "subsequently".
- Use "before" not "prior to".
- Use "after" not "following".

## What This Rule Does Not Change

- Code, file paths, identifiers, and source-code strings stay as written.
- Tool names and CLI commands stay as written.
- Mix task names (e.g. `mix format`, `mix test`) stay as written.

## Applies To

- All chat replies to the user.
- All prose in plan summaries, explanations, and status updates.
- All comments the user sees (e.g. commit messages, PR descriptions).
